  "raw": " that has not stopped some drivers from practicing it anyway, with the Thai team Sauber banned from running the rear wheels off their blundered 2011 wine car.\n\nTechnical director Mario Isola said Sauber had stopped using the tactic to retain rear end grip as it would cause the tyres to \"harden up\" and thereby reduce the rear downforce generated by the rears.\n\n\"We have decided not to do it any more,\" Isola told F1 Race Guide. \"They didn’t have any opportunities to start and run them and so we didn’t want to do it any more and we never do it.\n\n\"They made it easy for us to ban and take out so it was great. They (Red Bull) were worried about this and wanted to take them out and now they do.\"\n\nHe added: \"Sometimes you need to adapt for the tyres and sometimes if you don’t adapt and it almost comes to the rear",
  "serialized": "that has not stopped some drivers from practicing it anyway, with the Thai team Sauber banned from running the rear wheels off their blundered 2011 wine car. Technical director Mario Isola said Sauber had stopped using the tactic to retain rear end grip as it would cause the tyres to \"harden up\" and thereby reduce the rear downforce generated by the rears. \"We have decided not to do it any more,\" Isola told F1 Race Guide. \"They didn’t have any opportunities to start and run them and so we didn’t want to do it any more and we never do it. \"They made it easy for us to ban and take out so it was great. They (Red Bull) were worried about this and wanted to take them out and now they do.\" He added: \"Sometimes you need to adapt for the tyres and sometimes if you don’t adapt and it almost comes to the rear",
